---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PlannerCTA from '../../components/PlannerCTA.astro';

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I choose an electric or wood-burning sauna heater?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Electric heat is usually the more practical choice when code-compliant power is available and simple operation matters. Wood heat can fit remote sites or buyers who intentionally want fire operation, but it adds chimney, hearth, clearance, fuel, and local fire-code requirements."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What information do I need before choosing a sauna heater?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You need the finished room dimensions, glass and masonry area, insulation details, indoor or outdoor setting, available power or chimney path, desired controls, clearance plan, and the manual for the exact heater item."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---
<Base
  title="Sauna Heater Guide: Choose Electric or Wood Heat"
  description="Choose a sauna heater by room, fuel, controls, stone capacity, clearances, and complete installation scope before comparing exact models."
  canonical="/guides/best-sauna-heater"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-700 mb-3">Heater planning hub</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Choose a sauna heater by project, not by rank</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 mb-8">Last reviewed August 15, 2026</p>

    <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-8">
      <img src="/images/lifestyle/sauna-stones-closeup.jpg" alt="Close view of stones in a sauna heater" class="w-full object-cover max-h-80" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
    </div>

    <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-8 text-sm text-stone-700">
      <strong>Research boundary:</strong> This guide organizes the decision. It does not replace the room calculation, current manufacturer manual, licensed electrical work, chimney design, permits, or local inspection.
    </div>

    <p class="text-lg text-stone-700 mb-4">A heater is not a freestanding appliance choice. It is part of the room, power or chimney, controls, clearances, ventilation, and safety plan. Start with the project. The model shortlist comes later.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-8">The first decision is fuel. Electric and wood heaters can both produce traditional sauna heat and allow water on approved stones when the manual permits it. They create very different installation and operating responsibilities.</p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 mb-10">
      <section class="border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 bg-white">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-700 mb-2">Electric</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">For predictable controls and routine use</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 text-sm mb-4">Electric heat is usually the practical path when the site can support the exact voltage, phase, circuit, conductor, controls, and disconnect required by the heater manual.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 mb-5">
          <li>Built-in manual or separate digital controls.</li>
          <li>Wall-mounted or floor-standing formats.</li>
          <li>Dedicated electrical design and qualified installation.</li>
          <li>Potential remote operation only on approved packages.</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="/guides/best-electric-sauna-heaters/" class="text-amber-700 font-semibold hover:underline">Compare current electric formats</a>
      </section>

      <section class="border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 bg-white">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-700 mb-2">Wood-burning</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">For intentional fire operation</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 text-sm mb-4">Wood heat can fit remote sites or buyers who want to manage a fire. The heater is only one part of the system; the chimney, hearth, shielding, clearances, fuel, and fire code can drive the project.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 mb-5">
          <li>Approved chimney and roof or wall penetration.</li>
          <li>Combustible clearances and protective surfaces.</li>
          <li>Fuel storage, ash handling, and routine inspection.</li>
          <li>Local permit, fire, and insurance review.</li>
        </ul>
        <a href="/guides/best-wood-burning-sauna-heaters/" class="text-amber-700 font-semibold hover:underline">Compare wood-heater project requirements</a>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 bg-stone-50 mb-10">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-700 mb-2">Specialized gas option</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Propane requires a documented heater and an installer-led gas plan</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-4">Current US propane-capable sauna options are limited. Evaluate the exact room, fuel pressure, vent direction, combustion air, controls, rough-in, guard, permits, and licensed installation before treating gas as an electric or wood alternative.</p>
      <a href="/guides/propane-sauna-heater/" class="text-amber-700 font-semibold hover:underline">Review the propane project requirements</a>
    </div>

    <PlannerCTA
      source="sauna_heater_hub_fuel_comparison"
      heading="Build the room brief before selecting a model"
      description="Capture the site, dimensions, glass, power or chimney path, budget, delivery access, and installation needs in one shareable brief."
    />

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Six inputs that decide the heater</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4 text-stone-700 mb-8">
      <li><strong>Adjusted room volume.</strong> Measure the finished interior, then apply the selected manufacturer's method for glass, masonry, tile, and other uninsulated surfaces.</li>
      <li><strong>Fuel and utility path.</strong> Confirm panel capacity and cable route for electric, or chimney and combustion requirements for wood.</li>
      <li><strong>Controls.</strong> Decide whether built-in dials, a separate panel, scheduling, or approved remote use matters before comparing packages.</li>
      <li><strong>Placement and clearances.</strong> Wall, floor, bench embedding, guards, doors, and combustible surfaces limit the usable models.</li>
      <li><strong>Stone system.</strong> Compare the required stone size and quantity, whether stones are included, and the maintenance burden of a larger stone mass.</li>
      <li><strong>Complete cost.</strong> Add controls, sensors, power units, stones, guard, chimney or electrical work, permits, freight, and labor to the heater price.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do not use one universal sizing shortcut</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">Simple rules such as “one kilowatt per 45 cubic feet” can be an early sense check, but they are not a final selection method. Manufacturers publish different model ranges and adjustments. Glass, masonry, insulation, climate, ceiling height, and ventilation can change the load.</p>
    <p class="mb-8"><a href="/guides/sauna-heater-sizing-guide/" class="text-amber-700 font-semibold hover:underline">Use the heater-sizing planning guide</a></p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to send the electrician or installer</h2>
    <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-8">
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
        <li>The exact heater item number and current installation manual.</li>
        <li>Finished room dimensions and uninsulated surface calculations.</li>
        <li>Planned heater, control, sensor, guard, door, and bench locations.</li>
        <li>Panel or chimney route, distances, and access constraints.</li>
        <li>Local permit, inspection, and disconnect requirements.</li>
        <li>The retailer's itemized list of included and required components.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Should I choose electric or wood heat?</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700">Choose electric when routine controls and a code-compliant power path fit the site. Choose wood when fire operation is intentional and the chimney, hearth, clearances, fuel, and local requirements are acceptable.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">What information do I need before choosing a heater?</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700">You need the finished room dimensions, glass and masonry area, insulation, available power or chimney path, control preference, placement, and the manual for the exact heater item.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12 border-t border-stone-200 pt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Primary planning sources</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <li><a href="https://www.harvia.com/en-US/products/JH60BU1UL/kip60b-6-kw-240vac-steel-ul" rel="noopener" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Harvia KIP60B product documentation</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.harvia.com/en-US/products/WKM3/m3-black" rel="noopener" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Harvia M3 wood-heater documentation</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.harvia.com/hc/en-us/articles/19469322350866-Can-anyone-connect-an-electric-heater-to-the-power-grid" rel="noopener" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Harvia electrical-installation guidance</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Next steps</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/guides/best-electric-sauna-heaters/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Electric heater format comparison</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/best-wood-burning-sauna-heaters/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Wood-burning heater guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/propane-sauna-heater/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Review propane-heater constraints</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-electrical-requirements/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Electrical planning checklist</a></li>
        <li><a href="/brands/harvia-sauna-heater-review/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Harvia model-family guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/brands/huum-sauna-heater-review/?ref=best_sauna_heater&campaign=huum_package&placement=heater_next_steps" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline" data-track-event="internal_offer_click" data-cta-position="heater_next_steps_huum">HUUM DROP 6 and HIVE Mini 9 comparison</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
